(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-diuretics-k-sparing-cai-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Potassium-Sparing Diuretics, Aldosterone Antagonists & Carbonic Anhydrase Inhibitors Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which electrolyte disturbance sets potassium-sparing diuretics apart from other diuretic classes?","choices":["Hypokalemia","Hypomagnesemia","Hyperkalemia","Hypocalcemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The potassium-sparing diuretics raise serum potassium, the major difference from loops and thiazides. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which potassium-sparing diuretic is associated with azotemia?","choices":["Triamterene","Acetazolamide","Eplerenone","Amiloride"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Azotemia, a rise in blood urea nitrogen, is the reaction named for amiloride. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of action of amiloride and triamterene?","choices":["Inhibit the sodium-potassium-chloride carrier","Inhibit the sodium-chloride transporter","Block luminal sodium channels","Inhibit carbonic anhydrase"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Blocking luminal sodium channels in the collecting duct cuts sodium reabsorption and reduces the gradient that drives potassium secretion. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which pair of agents are the aldosterone antagonists?","choices":["Amiloride and triamterene","Spironolactone and eplerenone","Acetazolamide and dichlorphenamide","Metolazone and indapamide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Spironolactone and eplerenone are the aldosterone antagonists. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Triamterene is commonly combined in one product with which diuretic?","choices":["Furosemide","Hydrochlorothiazide","Spironolactone","Acetazolamide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Triamterene (and amiloride) are marketed in combination with hydrochlorothiazide, whose potassium loss the partner offsets. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which neurological disorder is an indication for carbonic anhydrase inhibitors?","choices":["Epilepsy","Parkinson disease","Multiple sclerosis","Meningitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The metabolic acidosis and central nervous system effects of these drugs make epilepsy an indication. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which acid-base disturbance do carbonic anhydrase inhibitors cause?","choices":["Metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Loss of bicarbonate in the urine causes a metabolic acidosis, unlike the contraction alkalosis of other diuretics. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which adverse effect in men is characteristic of spironolactone?","choices":["Gout","Gynecomastia","Megaloblastic anemia","Hearing loss"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Gynecomastia, or breast enlargement in men, is the characteristic sex-hormone effect of spironolactone, which blocks the androgen receptor. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which condition is an indication for an aldosterone antagonist?","choices":["Primary aldosteronism","Acute pulmonary edema","Mountain sickness","Calcium oxalate kidney stones"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Primary aldosteronism, an excess of aldosterone, is a direct indication for these drugs. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A man taking spironolactone develops breast enlargement. Which change in therapy is appropriate?","choices":["Switch to eplerenone","Add a thiazide diuretic","Switch to acetazolamide","Add a potassium supplement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Eplerenone has less effect on androgen receptors, so it causes less gynecomastia. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which agents are carbonic anhydrase inhibitors applied to the eye for glaucoma?","choices":["Timolol and betaxolol","Latanoprost and bimatoprost","Dorzolamide and brinzolamide","Pilocarpine and carbachol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dorzolamide and brinzolamide are the topical carbonic anhydrase inhibitors for glaucoma. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which two hormones control the collecting duct?","choices":["Renin and angiotensin II","Aldosterone and antidiuretic hormone","Insulin and glucagon","Parathyroid hormone and calcitonin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. These are the two hormones that act on the collecting duct, which is why it is the site of the potassium-sparing diuretics. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which diuretic classes deplete potassium?","choices":["Loops, thiazides and aldosterone antagonists","Amiloride, triamterene and spironolactone","Loops, thiazides and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors","Eplerenone and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. These three classes increase potassium excretion or deplete it. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which part of the nephron is the site of action of the potassium-sparing diuretics?","choices":["Proximal tubule","Collecting duct","Thick ascending limb","Distal convoluted tubule"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The collecting duct, where aldosterone and antidiuretic hormone act, is the site of the potassium-sparing diuretics. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which acid-base and potassium changes are the main adverse effects of aldosterone antagonists?","choices":["Hypokalemia and alkalosis","Hyperkalemia and alkalosis","Hypokalemia and acidosis","Hyperkalemia and mild acidosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Holding on to potassium causes hyperkalemia, along with a mild metabolic acidosis. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which laboratory abnormalities should be watched in a patient taking acetazolamide?","choices":["High bicarbonate and high potassium","Low bicarbonate and high potassium","Low bicarbonate and low potassium","High bicarbonate and low potassium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Metabolic acidosis lowers bicarbonate, and potassium depletion lowers potassium. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors lower pressure in which eye disease?","choices":["Cataract","Conjunctivitis","Dry eye","Glaucoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Reducing bicarbonate in the ciliary body lowers aqueous humor production, which treats glaucoma. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which process is reduced by carbonic anhydrase inhibitors?","choices":["Bicarbonate reabsorption","Glucose reabsorption","Chloride secretion","Calcium reabsorption"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Blocking carbonic anhydrase reduces bicarbonate reabsorption, along with hydrogen ion production and sodium-hydrogen exchange. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which condition raises aldosterone secondarily and is treated with an aldosterone antagonist?","choices":["Cirrhosis of the liver","Hypercalcemia","Mountain sickness","Glaucoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cirrhosis causes secondary hyperaldosteronism, and aldosterone antagonists are used for the resulting edema. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Besides edema, which cardiovascular conditions are indications for aldosterone antagonists?","choices":["Hypertension and heart failure","Stable angina and arrhythmia","Hyperlipidemia and claudication","Variant angina and stroke"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Aldosterone antagonists are used for hypertension and for heart failure, along with edematous conditions. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which pair of agents are the potassium-sparing diuretics that block luminal sodium channels?","choices":["Spironolactone and eplerenone","Acetazolamide and methazolamide","Furosemide and bumetanide","Amiloride and triamterene"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Amiloride and triamterene block sodium channels on the luminal side of the collecting duct, which holds potassium back. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which gastrointestinal symptoms are listed as adverse effects of aldosterone antagonists?","choices":["Constipation and bleeding","Nausea and vomiting","Dysphagia and drooling","Jaundice and pancreatitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nausea, vomiting and general gastrointestinal upset are listed for aldosterone antagonists. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 38"}]};
